    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> port-kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Shamra on the Syrian coast, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</li><li>Its scribes used a 30-sign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> alphabet and recorded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> cycle and the epics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-kirta">Kirta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat">Aqhat</a>.</li><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, it was destroyed by seaborne raiders c. 1185 BCE and never rebuilt.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many of Ugarit’s clay tablets survived because the fire that destroyed the city baked them hard; some of its last letters report enemy ships off the coast.</p></aside>
    </section>

