    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zimri-lim.jpg" alt="Zimri-Lim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zimrilim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jolle, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit stood on the mound of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Shamra, about a kilometre from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> north of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a>, a site settled since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the early second millennium BCE it became the capital of a coastal kingdom, served by the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minet-el-beida">Minet el-Beida</a> and backed by a fertile plain and wooded hills. Later kings traced their dynasty to a founder named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqaru">Yaqaru</a>, whose old cylinder seal they continued to use on royal documents, but little is known of him or his date. Ugarit enters the historical record in the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, which describe King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> travelling to the city around 1765 BCE and show it as a place where merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> sought Mediterranean goods. Egyptian objects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> found at the site indicate early contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as well. The kingdom’s territory, roughly the size of a modern district of some 2,000 square kilometres, included scores of villages and a population perhaps in the tens of thousands. Its history was rediscovered by chance in 1928, when a farmer ploughing near Minet el-Beida broke into a tomb; excavations led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-schaeffer">Claude Schaeffer</a> from 1929 uncovered the city and its archives.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baal.jpg" alt="Baal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baal_thunderbolt_Louvre_AO15775.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jastrow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit was one of the great trading cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, a meeting point for merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Its ships and merchants carried grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, purple-dyed textiles and metals, and the city traded closely with Alashiya on Cyprus, the main source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. Wealthy merchants such as Urtenu and Rapanu kept their own archives in large town houses, and Hittite edicts regulated the activities of traders from Ura in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> who worked in the city. The royal palace, with some ninety rooms, courtyards and a garden, was among the largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Society was divided between the king and his dependants, known in Ugaritic as the “men of the king”, who received land or rations for service, and the village communities that paid taxes and provided labour. Religion centred on a pantheon headed by El and the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, whose temples stood on the city’s acropolis beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>. Ugarit’s scribes worked in at least eight languages and five scripts, including Akkadian, Hurrian, Hittite, Egyptian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypro-minoan-syllabary">Cypro-Minoan</a> and their own Ugaritic, written in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> alphabet of thirty signs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="Battle of Kadesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Abou_Simbel6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit was a wealthy rather than a military power, and its kings preserved the kingdom chiefly through diplomacy and alliances with stronger states. Its army relied on chariotry manned by the <em>maryannu</em>, an elite class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warriors found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, together with infantry raised from the villages. The city was protected by a stone rampart and a fortified gate with a sloping glacis. Ugarit also maintained a fleet, which was significant enough for the Hittite kings to call on it. Around the 1340s BCE neighbouring kingdoms, including Mukish, Niya and Nuhashshe, attacked Ugarit when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-ii">Niqmaddu II</a> refused to join their coalition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>; he appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>, who drove the attackers off and rewarded Ugarit with territory. As a Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> Ugarit had to send troops to its overlord’s wars, and Egyptian accounts list it among the allies of the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a> in 1274 BCE. In the final crisis these obligations proved fatal: letters of King Ammurapi report that his troops were away in the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> and his ships in Lukka when enemy ships arrived off the coast and burned his towns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i.jpg" alt="Suppiluliuma I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Hittite_rule_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sémhur; derivative work by Ikonact, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit was a hereditary monarchy in which the king presided over the palace administration, justice and foreign relations, assisted by a queen mother who could wield considerable influence, by high officials such as the prefect, and by a corps of trained scribes. Legal documents recording grants of land, sales, adoptions and freedom from obligations were drawn up in Akkadian and sealed with the dynastic seal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqaru">Yaqaru</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-period">Amarna age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-i">Ammishtamru I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-ii">Niqmaddu II</a> corresponded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, but around 1350 BCE Niqmaddu II accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>, sealing a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> that fixed an annual tribute in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and fine garments. Thereafter Hittite authority was exercised largely through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of Carchemish, who acted as judge in disputes between Ugarit and its neighbours. Niqmaddu II’s successors included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arhalba">Arhalba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmepa-of-ugarit">Niqmepa</a>, whose long reign saw the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyannu">Siyannu</a> detached from Ugarit by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-ii">Ammishtamru II</a>’s divorce from a princess of Amurru became an international affair settled by Hittite verdict. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibiranu">Ibiranu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-iii">Niqmaddu III</a> and Ammurapi, the last king, ruled in the troubled late thirteenth and early twelfth centuries BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat.jpg" alt="Tale of Aqhat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat">Tale of Aqhat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danel_epic_AO17323_img_0160.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rama, CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit’s greatest legacy lies in the tablets found in its palace, temples and private houses. Its scribes developed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> alphabet of thirty signs, impressed on clay like Mesopotamian cuneiform but working on the alphabetic principle invented earlier in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>; abecedary tablets show its letters in an order close to that of the later Phoenician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> alphabets. In this script the scribe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilimilku">Ilimilku</a>, traditionally dated to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-ii">Niqmaddu II</a> though some scholars place him later, copied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> cycle, which tells of the storm god’s battles with Yam (Sea) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mot-god">Mot</a> (Death), and the epics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-kirta">Kirta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-aqhat">Aqhat</a>. These texts transformed the study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, revealing the Canaanite religious world that biblical writers knew, together with shared poetic forms, divine titles and imagery such as the cloud-rider and the defeat of the sea monster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotan">Lotan</a>, the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leviathan">Leviathan</a>. The archives in Akkadian and other languages are equally valuable for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> diplomacy, law and trade, documenting how a small kingdom worked within the international system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. Ugarit’s sudden destruction also makes it a key witness to the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> world around 1200 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ugarit’s end came during the wider crisis of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> around 1200 BCE. In its final decades the Hittite kingdom, itself suffering from famine, pressed Ugarit for grain shipments and military aid, and letters show Hittite kings complaining about delays. The archives found in the ruins record the approach of disaster: Ammurapi wrote to the king of Alashiya that enemy ships had come, burned his cities and done harm in his country while his own forces were absent, and other letters warn of enemy vessels sighted at sea. A letter from Bay, a powerful official at the Egyptian court, dates the last phase of the kingdom to the years after about 1192 BCE. Shortly afterwards, around 1185 BCE, the city was sacked and burned, probably by seaborne raiders of the kind Egyptian sources call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, although famine, earthquakes and the collapse of the Hittite state may all have contributed. Letters apparently never sent, among them tablets once thought by the excavators to have been found in an oven awaiting baking, suggest how suddenly the end came. The city was never rebuilt and only slight later occupation followed, while the coastal region passed to small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> communities.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ugarit was a wealthy port-kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> Syrian coast, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, whose archives, alphabetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> myths illuminate Canaanite religion and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, and whose destruction around 1185 BCE marks the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> world.</em></p>

